    guide: [{ heading: "What an EMI is", body: "An Equated Monthly Instalment (EMI) is the fixed monthly payment that repays a home, car, or personal loan over its tenure. Each EMI splits into interest on the outstanding balance plus principal repayment, so early payments are interest-heavy and later payments reduce principal faster. Our EMI calculator covers fixed-rate amortizing loans in India and globally, showing monthly EMI, total interest, total payable, and a yearly schedule so borrowers can compare tenures before approaching a lender." }, { heading: "How EMI is calculated", body: "Lenders use EMI = P×r×(1+r)^n/((1+r)^n-1), where P is principal, r is monthly rate (annual÷12÷100), and n is months. For example, Rs 10,00,000 at 9% for 20 years gives r=0.0075 and n=240, producing an EMI near Rs 8,997. Total payable is EMI×n, about Rs 21.59 lakh, so total interest is about Rs 11.59 lakh. A zero interest rate falls back to P÷n, while extra principal payments shorten tenure and reduce interest directly." }, { heading: "Worked example and limitations", body: "Take Rs 10 lakh at 9% for 20 years: you pay Rs 8,997 monthly, Rs 21.59 lakh total, and Rs 11.59 lakh interest over 240 payments. Shortening to 15 years raises EMI to about Rs 10,143 but cuts interest to roughly Rs 8.26 lakh, saving over Rs 3.3 lakh. This is an educational estimate only, excluding processing fees, insurance, floating-rate resets, and prepayment penalties. Actual lender schedules may differ by rounding. Confirm with your sanction letter. See /terms." }]
